---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { site, description, pageTitle } from '../../content';
import { SEGMENTS as SEGS } from '../../data/segments';

const path = '/for';
const title = pageTitle(site, 'for');
const desc = description(site, 'for');
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <section class="py-14 md:py-20">
    <div class="container">
      <h1 class="hero-title text-5xl md:text-6xl">Shopify SMS apps by store type</h1>
      <p class="mt-6 max-w-3xl text-xl text-cyan-100">Choose a playbook by the decision your customer needs to make: finishing a personalized order, choosing a subscription edition, or reordering a consumable. A store category is a starting point for that decision, not a universal texting schedule.</p>
      <div class="mt-10 grid gap-4 md:grid-cols-2 lg:grid-cols-3">
        {SEGS.map((s) => (
          <a class="card" href={`/for/${s.slug}`}>
            <h2 class="text-xl font-black text-white">{s.name}</h2>
            <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-cyan-100">Open the {s.name} playbook.</p>
          </a>
        ))}
      </div>
      <article class="prose-shopify card mt-12 max-w-4xl">
        <h2>Choose by operating constraint</h2>
        <p>For a mixed catalog, classify the order or workflow before assigning the whole customer to one category. Someone buying a gift once and food repeatedly needs different follow-up for each purchase. Keep gift recipient details separate from the purchaser's marketing preferences.</p>
        <ul>
          <li><a href="/for/jewelry" class="link">Jewelry</a>: start here when engraving, sizing, an advisor conversation or a gift deadline determines whether an order can proceed.</li>
          <li><a href="/for/print-on-demand" class="link">Print on demand</a>: use this for unfinished artwork, proof approval, blank availability and production capacity. A catalog stock count alone cannot resolve a proof problem.</li>
          <li><a href="/for/pet-products" class="link">Pet products</a>: use this for household and SKU consumption questions. A previous order date alone does not establish when supplies run out.</li>
          <li><a href="/for/subscription-boxes" class="link">Subscription boxes</a>: use this when the current contract, edition, skip or prepaid gift status determines the next action. A past box purchase does not establish an active contract.</li>
        </ul>
        <h2>Turn the playbook into a provider brief</h2>
        <p>Write down the event needed, where its latest value lives, the person allowed to receive the message, and what cancels it. Ask a provider to demonstrate that complete path before choosing an app. An order synchronization feature does not by itself establish that proof approval, household consumption or contract state is available to a flow.</p>
        <p>For example, a proposed mixed-store demonstration could place a one-off gift order beside a recurring box contract. Request a skip, change the gift address and complete a separate cart. Check that each action updates only its intended workflow and that a queued message is rechecked before sending. This is a suggested evaluation, not a test we ran.</p>
        <h2>Budget for the decision, not just the text</h2>
        <p>Request a quote using your destination mix, expected billable message units, media use and peak traffic. Include platform charges, carrier charges, discounts, fulfillment and reply handling in the decision. Compare the cost of resolving the actual blocker with the contribution from fulfilled orders after returns; attributed revenue alone cannot establish incremental profit.</p>
        <p>Assign an owner for replies and failed state updates. Decide how overlapping email and SMS workflows will choose the next message and cancel stale ones. Use email for detail when that suits the customer's preferences; a separate email platform is optional. These category guides do not establish a provider ranking or guarantee program compliance.</p>
        <h2>Resolve launch dependencies separately</h2>
        <p>A useful category match does not authorize a send. Confirm the program's market-specific legal requirements, recipient permissions, opt-out handling and timing controls with the responsible specialists and provider. The linked playbooks are editorial planning aids; their examples do not prove that a particular integration exists in your account.</p>
        <p>After selecting the workflow, open the <a href="/apps" class="link">app catalog</a> with your provider brief. Keep unresolved data access, pricing and operational responsibilities on the evaluation list rather than treating a category recommendation as approval to launch.</p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
